I’ll first inspect the specimen metadata, diffraction grid, and the frozen phase-index/refinement utilities. Then I’ll identify all coherent peak families, build a multiphase GSAS-II model, and verify that the exported CIFs, profile, fractions, and project are mutually consistent.

The chemistry points first to wolframite-type NiWO₄, with NiO and WO₃ as the most plausible residual phases. I’m now testing that ordinary inventory against the full measured peak family and will only expand the search if coherent reflections remain unassigned.

The peak-family check supports a two-phase model: wolframite NiWO₄ accounts for the dominant 19.2°, 24.9°, 30.9°, 36.5°, 41.6°, and 54.6° families, while monoclinic WO₃ accounts for the resolved 23.05/23.54/24.29° triplet and the 33.20/33.52/34.09° family. I see no broad-family support for NiO at this stage; its strongest 43.4° line is absent. I’m moving this two-phase inventory into GSAS-II and refining before judging weaker residuals.

The two-phase refinement is already at Rwp ≈ 5.4%, with positive scales corresponding roughly to three-quarters NiWO₄ and one-quarter WO₃. The remaining residuals sit on accepted reflections as paired positive/negative lobes, so I’m treating them as structure/profile mismatch—not adding a speculative third phase. I’m also constraining broadening and calibration to the stated physical ranges before refining coordinates.

A structural screen caught an important issue: the best-positioned 1960 WO₃ reference has an implausible 1.57 Å O–O contact. I’m therefore holding the NiWO₄ refinement, which remains physically sound, and comparing the other exact-composition WO₃ references. This is precisely the case where a modest profile advantage should not override bad geometry.

The replacement WO₃ model (COD 2311041, P2₁/c) is both physically sound and fits better than the rejected reference. The current physical model has Rwp ≈ 4.15–4.27% depending on background flexibility, GOF near the counting-statistics limit, Ni–O ≈ 1.98–2.05 Å, NiWO₄ W–O ≈ 1.85–2.20 Å, and WO₃ W–O ≈ 1.69–2.19 Å. Preferred orientation and surface roughness tests were unsupported; I’m now consolidating only the supported thermal/profile terms on the full measured grid.

The required deliverables are complete and independently replay cleanly. I’m doing one final consistency audit now—checking the submitted CIF rounding against the project, the complete fit-grid format, and whether the smooth background choice is stable rather than noise-driven.

Identified two crystalline phases:

- NiWO₄ (wolframite, P2/c): 77.02 wt%
- WO₃ (monoclinic, P2₁/c): 22.98 wt%

Full-grid fit: Rwp 4.1438%, Rp 3.1994%, GOF 1.195. Independent zero-cycle replay reproduced the profile and fractions with no physical-review flags. Fractions carry roughly 1–2 wt% systematic uncertainty; amorphous content is not quantified.

